Content-Type: application/issue
ID: 1
Type: feature
Title: Stub preempt_schedule_thunk in test/mock.c so unit tests link against vanilla kernels
Status: open
Priority: medium
Created: 2026-10-02
Module: Module
Relationships: 
Description: PROPOSAL — make the unit tests linkable against clean vanilla
             kernel.org trees, not only the running distro kernel.
             
             ## Problem
             `test/unit` links cleanly when KDIR points at a distro kernel (the
             test/Makefile default, /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build) but FAILS to
             link against a clean vanilla kernel.org tree. Against vanilla
             x86_64 (defconfig):
             
             ld: undefined reference to `preempt_schedule_thunk'
             (also `preempt_schedule_notrace_thunk')
             
             referenced from the inlined preempt_enable() in <linux/rcupdate.h>,
             <linux/bit_spinlock.h>, <linux/rhashtable.h> — i.e. from
             homa_peer.o, rhashtable.o, unit_homa_peer.o.
             
             ## Root cause
             With CONFIG_HAVE_STATIC_CALL_INLINE=y (set on vanilla x86
             defconfig), the static-call machinery pins each static call's
             *default target* as an addressable symbol. For `preempt_schedule`
             that default target is `preempt_schedule_thunk`
             (arch/x86/entry/thunk.S). mock.c already stubs the trampoline
             `__SCT__preempt_schedule`, but not the underlying thunk, so the
             reference is unresolved. Distro kernels don't surface it
             identically, so the gap is invisible when building against the
             running kernel.
             
             ## Proposed change
             Add two no-op stubs to test/mock.c, next to the existing
             `__SCT__preempt_schedule` stub:
             
             void preempt_schedule_thunk(void) {}
             void preempt_schedule_notrace_thunk(void) {}
             
             They are no-ops in the userspace harness, consistent with the
             trampoline stub already there.
             
             ## Benefit
             Lets the unit tests build and run against arbitrary vanilla
             kernel.org trees, which enables per-kernel-version compatibility
             testing. Removes the need for a downstream shim: an out-of-tree
             test battery currently concatenates exactly these two stubs onto
             its own copy of mock.c (unit-mock-compat.c) to work around this.
             
             ## Acceptance
             `make -C test KDIR=<vanilla defconfig tree>` builds and runs `unit`
             with no external shim and no undefined-symbol link errors.
             
             ## Out of scope (context only)
             Running against vanilla also requires the kernel configured with
             NUMA on, so MAX_NUMNODES >= 2 (mock_cpu_to_node() returns node 0/1
             and homa_tx_pool_init() BUG_ON()s otherwise) and so x86 keeps
             cpu_to_node a macro that mock.h's redirect catches. That is a
             config choice on the consumer side, not a mock.c change.
